    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Reports and exported tables (page 5)\"\n---\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n:::\n\n\nPage 5 of the app, **Report export**, gives you the results to keep: two reports and the tables\nof the data. Everything on this page uses one **final normalization method**.\n\n## Final normalization method\n\nChoose the method first; the buttons stay greyed out until you do. **Best** is the method with the\nlowest median RSD% among the QC replicates (here Multivariate (PCA)), and each option shows\nits median RSD%:\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"datatables html-widget html-fill-item\" id=\"htmlwidget-16444ac52cd4d424919b\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto;\"></div>\n<script type=\"application/json\" data-for=\"htmlwidget-16444ac52cd4d424919b\">{\"x\":{\"filter\":\"none\",\"vertical\":false,\"data\":[[\"Best: Multivariate (PCA)\",\"Without normalization\",\"LOESS\",\"Closest RT\",\"Multivariate (PCA)\"],[26.5,31.5,27.8,28.6,26.5]],\"container\":\"<table class=\\\"display\\\">\\n  <thead>\\n    <tr>\\n      <th>Option<\\/th>\\n      <th>Median RSD%<\\/th>\\n    <\\/tr>\\n  <\\/thead>\\n<\\/table>\",\"options\":{\"dom\":\"t\",\"paging\":false,\"scrollX\":true,\"scrollY\":\"240px\",\"scrollCollapse\":true,\"columnDefs\":[{\"className\":\"dt-center\",\"targets\":\"_all\"},{\"name\":\"Option\",\"targets\":0},{\"name\":\"Median RSD%\",\"targets\":1}],\"order\":[],\"autoWidth\":false,\"orderClasses\":false}},\"evals\":[],\"jsHooks\":[]}</script>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nChoose another method when, for example, 3.8 shows that it works clearly better for the compounds\nyou are most interested in.\n\n## Reports\n\n| Button | Report | For |\n|---|---|---|\n| **QC/QA report (html)** | the internal report: all quality-control steps, tables and interactive figures, as on pages 2–4 of the app | checking and documenting the processing |\n| **Summary report (pdf)** | the external report: the project, the methods and the results in a few pages | sharing the results |\n\nThe buttons are in the box **Report** at the top of the page, next to the normalization method. A\nprogress bar shows how far the report is. When it is ready, it is shown in **Preview report**, the\nwide box below, in its own tab: *QC/QA report (html)* or *Summary report (pdf)*, each with its download\nbutton in the upper right corner (**Download HTML report**, **Download PDF report**). The reports\nname the settings that were used, so they document the processing.\n\n## Exported tables\n\n**Export data into tables**, in the box **Export tabular data** at the top right, writes five Excel files for the final method (and each of their sheets\nas a `.csv` file), ready to download:\n\n| File | Content |\n|---|---|\n| `….norm.xlsx` | normalized peak areas of all features (also non-targeted) |\n| `….norm_blk.xlsx` | the same after blank subtraction |\n| `….conc.xlsx` | concentrations of the targeted compounds: QCs, study samples and blanks |\n| `….conc_all.xlsx` | the same with all samples, including the calibration samples |\n| `….history.xlsx` | what the processing did with each feature and sample (see [Processing history](#processing-history)) |\n\nOn SciLifeLab Serve, very large tables (more than 5 million values, e.g. tens of thousands of\nnon-targeted features) are written as `.csv` files only, with the same layout, since an Excel file\nof that size takes too much memory to create there. This concerns `….norm` and `….norm_blk`, and\nthe export box says so in advance. Run locally, QuaNTA always writes the Excel files as well.\n\nThis is the start of a batch sheet of the concentration table of the example\n(QuaNTA_example_ESIpos.mvis.conc.xlsx), the feature columns first and then the samples:\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"datatables html-widget html-fill-item\" id=\"htmlwidget-ad4996e0a7426b9bb7f0\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto;\"></div>\n<script type=\"application/json\" data-for=\"htmlwidget-ad4